  "slug": "colored-indexes-832d",
  "summary": "Interpretability and consensus-selection methods now examine how structure predictors use internal representations and how candidate structure pools are chosen.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on machine-learning models for biomolecular sequence and structure, with a shift toward examining how these models work and how their outputs are selected. AlphaInterp reports that a few divergent MSA homologues suffice to recover AlphaFold 3 structure, linking this to pairformer latent geometry. A separate preprint probes OpenFold's 48 Evoformer layers with four decoders, tracking distance decodability, TM-score reconstruction, and triangle-inequality violations. On the prediction side, AbPoolRank ranks mixed antibody-antigen structure pools by cross-pose agreement and geometry, improving prediction. FoldMapper builds structure-derived evolutionary profiles for inverse folding through shape-based homologue retrieval and a multiscale graph encoder. Infernal-GPU accelerates covariance-model dynamic programming on GPUs, recalibrating Rfam E-values and recovering remote structured-RNA homologs. What is new across these is quantitative internal probing of structure predictors and predictor-agnostic consensus selection, rather than a single shared algorithm.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
